WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:01.940
LegalEagle is coming in loud and clear.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.120
Thanks to Ting Mobile.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.390
The president has been
diagnosed with COVID-19

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.090
and has been flown to
Walter Reed Medical Center.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.880
His condition may be worsening.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.330
This has huge implications
for not just the way

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:14.810
that the government is run

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.130
but also the 2020 election.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:18.620
Now we all know that president Trump

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.220
mocked Hillary Clinton's pneumonia-

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:24.010
- She's supposed to fight
all of these different things

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:26.610
and she can't make it 15 feet to her car.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.670
Give me a break.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.600
- Made fun of Biden for
allegedly hiding from

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:31.140
the Coronavirus in his basement.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:33.300
- Joe Biden is sleeping
or whatever he's doing

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:35.890
in his basement, he doesn't
seem to come out a lot.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:38.050
- And made fun of Biden
for wearing a mask.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.170
- Did you ever see a man that
likes a mask as much as him?

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:43.600
- And made fun of Biden
wearing a mask again.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.020
- I don't wear a mask like him.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.410
Every time you see him, he's got a mask.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.697
He could be speaking
200 feet away from you

00:00:48.697 --> 00:00:51.300
and he shows up with the
biggest mask I've ever seen.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.500
- So let me acknowledge
the elephant in the room.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.790
There may be some shouting
parade today from people

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:57.450
who are gleeful about this development.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.880
I'm not one of those people.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.090
COVID-19 is a serious apolitical
virus that isn't selective

00:01:02.090 --> 00:01:03.030
about who it infects.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.890
And I wouldn't wish it on anyone.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.100
Many people who work in the
White House or Congress,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.650
or attended fundraisers or
debates with Trump's staff.

00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.050
Were also exposed and we should
hope that they all recover

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.490
with no complications.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.230
However, this is truly unprecedented

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:16.460
for many different reasons.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.530
First of all, the major
presidential candidates

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.250
both are over the age of 70
Joe Biden and Donald Trump

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:23.760
are both in the high risk
category for health complications

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.340
from COVID-19.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.660
And it would be irresponsible
not to consider what happens,

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.040
not only to the campaigns, but
also the country as a whole,

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.900
if either of them dies
or becomes incapacitated.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:35.690
Especially since president
Trump was diagnosed

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.280
with Coronavirus just 33
days before the election.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.970
How will American democracy handle

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:41.140
this earth shattering event?

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.240
What if the president is incapacitated

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.250
or worse before the election?

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.290
And what happens between the election

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:49.105
and the Electoral College?

00:01:49.105 --> 00:01:51.855
(trumpet blares)

00:01:53.116 --> 00:01:54.980
Hey LegalEagles, it's time
to think like a lawyer

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:56.320
and a doctor.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.660
The president has Coronavirus
and all four candidates

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:00.950
for president and vice president
are mixing and mingling

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.200
with the American people
during a pandemic.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.260
How panicked should we be
about something happening

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.260
to one of them?

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.970
Well, we have to start
with the 25th Amendment,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:11.390
the U.S Constitution,
original plan for what happens

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.020
in the event of a presidential disability

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.080
or inability to work, was
for the vice president

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:15.913
to take over.

00:02:15.913 --> 00:02:18.320
This seems simple enough,
but Article 2, Section 1

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.590
Clause 6, doesn't exactly
explain how to decide

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.730
when a president is disabled or unable

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.430
to perform the duties of the office.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.430
Congress passed laws to
clarify the procedure

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:29.680
but nothing was formalized
until the 25th Amendment

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.700
was ratified in 1967.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.340
So let's talk about Section 3.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:35.840
Section 3 of the 25th Amendment
provides the president

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.960
with the authority to
declare him or herself

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.460
unable to discharge the powers
and duties of the office.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.020
If the president makes a declaration,

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:44.080
this officially transfers
authority of the office

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.930
to the vice president who exercises it

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.430
as the acting president,
until the president reclaims

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.170
this authority by declaring
the disability to be ended.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.330
This could apply if the
president was having surgery.

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:54.270
For example.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.410
The key aspect of Section
3 is that the president

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:59.330
has discretion over when it
applies and when it ends.

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.210
This has happened a couple times before.

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:04.030
Ronald Reagan sent a letter
suggesting that he was invoking

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:06.650
Section 3 when he underwent
surgery for a polyp.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:08.760
George H. W. Bush was the acting president

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:11.660
for about eight hours after
which Reagan resumed duties.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.250
When George W. Bush was
president, he formally invoked

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.610
Section 3 twice when he was anesthetized

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.410
for routine medical procedures.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:21.760
As vice president, Dick Cheney
took over on both occasions.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.220
So hypothetically let's say
president Trump declares

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:25.950
that he will be unable
to perform the duties

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:28.750
of his office during his
Coronavirus quarantine.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.750
We know Mike Pence would
be acting president.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.190
But there's one additional
wrinkle to consider.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.130
Most scholars believe that
he would not be allowed

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.950
to continue in his role as
president of the senate.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.037
According to the constitution,
"The Senate shall choose

00:03:40.037 --> 00:03:43.217
"their other Officers, and
also a President pro tempore,

00:03:43.217 --> 00:03:44.507
"in the absence of the Vice President,

00:03:44.507 --> 00:03:46.827
"or when he shall exercise
the Office of President

00:03:46.827 --> 00:03:48.000
"of the United States."

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.990
Under such a scenario,
the president pro tempore

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:52.080
would exercise the powers
and duties as the president

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.740
of the Senate for the
duration of disability.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.680
The vice president would
resume both the title

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.820
and duties of president of
the Senate, once the president

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:01.080
declared his disability over.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.380
Which brings us to Section
4 of the 25th Amendment,

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.610
which is far more complicated.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.120
You've probably seen RESISTANCE Twitter

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.010
kicking around the idea
that the president's cabinet

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.980
invoking the 25th amendment,
because they allege

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.090
Trump is in the mental decline.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:14.610
There is in fact a process for that

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.050
but it's never happened
and it's highly political

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.770
and unlikely to happen.

00:04:18.770 --> 00:04:20.380
Section 4 provides for instances

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.030
of Contingent Presidential Disability.

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:24.430
This means that there may be
situations where a president

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:27.040
is unable to discharge
the duties of office,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.160
but is unwilling or unable to declare

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.090
the disability under Section 3.

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:32.870
So if the president was suddenly taken ill

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:35.500
or put on a ventilator or
had an emergency surgery

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.290
or went into a coma, then
the 25th Amendment applies

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.180
because the president
wouldn't have the ability

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:42.850
to make that determination for themselves.

00:04:42.850 --> 00:04:44.900
However, the authors of
the Amendment also wanted

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:46.940
to cover scenarios where the
president thinks he or she

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:49.430
is totally fine, but the
vice president and a majority

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:52.020
of the cabinet think that
he or she is not fine.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:55.070
Remember America is the country
that was created in part

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.640
because colonists didn't want
to be ruled by King George

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.950
the Third, who was said to
be suffering from madness.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:04.770
So if the president is
unwilling to declare themselves

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.470
incapacitated, then the
cabinet and vice president

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.700
may act under these
circumstances, this section

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.100
authorize the vice president
and the majority of either

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.580
the cabinet or any other
body established by law,

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.830
acting jointly, to declare
the president to be disabled.

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:20.960
The vice president would need
the concurrence of at least

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.060
eight of the 15 Cabinet Officers
to activate the Declaration

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:25.580
of Presidential Disability.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.560
Now, once they notify
the president pro tempore

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.570
and the speaker,

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.980
the vice president immediately
assumes the powers and duties

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.640
of the office as acting president.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:34.850
Now, the drafters of the
Amendment were well aware

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:37.180
that this opened up the
possibility of a president

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.780
being removed solely
for political reasons.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.857
Therefore, the amendment gives
the president a way back in.

00:05:42.857 --> 00:05:45.267
"If the President in a time
of his choice transmits

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:47.217
"a written message to the
president pro tempore and

00:05:47.217 --> 00:05:49.197
"the Speaker that no disability exists,

00:05:49.197 --> 00:05:51.010
"he or she resumes office."

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.640
This sets up a political showdown
because the vice president

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:55.860
and majority of the cabinet
have four days to contest

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:58.280
this finding, if they think
the president isn't capable

00:05:58.280 --> 00:05:59.113
of serving.

00:05:59.113 --> 00:06:01.340
And then Congress decides.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:03.840
If a two thirds vote of
both Houses believe that

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.330
the president is unable to
discharge the duties of office.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:08.660
The vice-president continues
as acting president

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.040
until the disability is resolved.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.050
If a two thirds margin is
not obtained, or if Congress

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:17.030
is in session, but at
the time does not vote

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:20.010
on the question within 21 days
of receiving the declaration.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.420
Then the president resumes the
powers and duties of office.

00:06:22.420 --> 00:06:25.330
And similarly, if Congress
is not in session at the time

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.510
and assembles but doesn't
vote within 21 days

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.620
then the president resumes
the powers and duties

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:30.840
of the office.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.010
Like I said, "This is complicated."

00:06:33.010 --> 00:06:35.360
And Section 4 has not been
activated since the Amendment

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.140
was ratified in 1967.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.310
But there are two events
during Reagan's presidency

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:41.690
where it arguably should
have been invoked.

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:44.340
On March 30th, 1981,
president Reagan was shot

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.670
and seriously wounded while
leaving a speaking engagement

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:47.503
in Washington.

00:06:47.503 --> 00:06:50.430
Actually just down the street
at the Hilton on Connecticut.

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:53.280
He had an emergency surgery
and he was under anesthesia.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.250
His aides debated whether
or not to invoke Section 4.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:58.470
They were notified that a
full recovery was expected

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.190
and decided no further
action needed to be taken.

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:03.820
When president Reagan was
shot, Al Haig his Secretary

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:05.870
of state made the big
mistake of telling the press,

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.487
that he was in control while the president

00:07:07.487 --> 00:07:09.860
was in surgery.
(indistinct chatter)

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.980
- As of now, I am in control
here in the White House,

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:15.453
pending return of the vice president.

00:07:15.453 --> 00:07:17.900
And I'm in close touch with him.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:20.450
If something came up, I will
check with him, of course.

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.810
- This was considered a faux
pas demonstrating ignorance

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.010
of the constitution by
a high ranking official

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.460
which made the government
look particularly bad.

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:30.140
And although people
remember Al Haig's comment,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.670
few people are even
aware that the other side

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.900
of Section 4 was also in
play during his presidency.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:38.270
Reagan's mental status was
also questioned by staff

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:39.370
when he was in office.

00:07:39.370 --> 00:07:42.810
In 1987, former senator Howard
Baker, president Reagan's

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.850
newly appointed chief of
staff, reportedly received

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:47.247
a memorandum from an aide
that claimed the president,

00:07:47.247 --> 00:07:49.490
"Was inattentive and inept."

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.040
The memorandum went on
to urge Baker to consider

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.350
the possibility of Section
4 of the 25th Amendment

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:55.670
might be applied.

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:58.370
Baker concluded, "The president
was attentive and alert."

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:01.160
And at a March 2nd meeting
dismissed the report.

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:03.530
Five years after president
Reagan left the presidency,

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:05.310
he was diagnosed with Alzheimer's.

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.310
And we don't know definitively
whether he suffered

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:09.330
from the disease while he was in office.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:12.030
But what happens to the presidency
in the line of succession

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:14.370
is pretty simple in
comparison to what happens

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:16.530
if a candidate dies before the election.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:17.860
And that is the big question

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:19.990
on many people's minds right now.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:21.820
We don't have a Federal law that specifies

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:23.630
what happens if a candidate
for president dies

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:24.620
before the election.

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.107
In the absence of a Federal law saying,

00:08:26.107 --> 00:08:28.410
"If a candidate dies, we do X, Y, and Z."

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:31.520
We have to look to several
places to determine what happens.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.180
We look at the rules of
the political parties,

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:35.300
State law and cases, which are similar.

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.950
But I warn you that this is
largely uncharted territory

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:41.310
because we've never had a
presidential candidate die right

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:42.990
before an election in the modern era.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.970
And we don't have any Supreme
court cases directly on point.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:48.910
So let's first examine
what the political parties

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:50.680
have to say about this issue.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.830
The Democratic National
Committee has a clear rule

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.000
for the situation.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:57.490
The 447 members of the
DNC would meet and choose

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:58.580
their new nominee.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:02.320
The DNC chair currently Tom
Perez is required to consult

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.420
with the Democratic leadership
in Congress and with the

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:06.300
Democratic Governors Association.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:08.710
After the consultation,
the chair provides a report

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:11.500
to the DNC members who
then make the choice.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:14.230
Presumably in this case,
they would pick Kamala Harris

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:17.850
if Joe Biden were incapacitated or other.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:19.950
But they're not required to do so.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:22.310
The Republican National
Committee has similar rules.

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.700
The RNC has 168 members,
three from each state

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.670
plus three from six territories.

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:29.680
The members would revolt
in a similar manner

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:31.080
as they did at the convention.

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:33.230
If those three members of a
state disagree with each other,

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:35.740
then they each get to cast
one third of the votes

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:36.650
of that state.

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:39.570
And again, they might pick
Mike Pence in this situation,

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:40.930
but they're not obligated to put him

00:09:40.930 --> 00:09:42.130
at the top of the ticket.

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.210
As an example, consider
the 1972 general election

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:47.000
when the Democratic Party filled a vacancy

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.530
because vice presidential
nominee, Senator Thomas Eagleton

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.800
resigned at the end of July.

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:55.030
The Democratic National
Committee met on August 8th

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:57.020
to nominate R. Sargent Shriver

00:09:57.020 --> 00:09:59.080
as the new vice presidential candidate.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.380
So, so far so good, right?

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:02.940
I mean, the parties would
just decide who they wanted

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:04.660
and the voters would go vote.

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:06.010
Well, actually, no.

00:10:06.010 --> 00:10:07.830
It's way more complicated than that.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:10.160
The parties would still
need to replace the name

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:12.410
of their dead or incapacitated candidate

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.780
on each state's ballot with
that of the new candidate.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.350
The constitution gives the
states the right to set the time,

00:10:18.350 --> 00:10:20.810
place and manner over federal elections.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:22.850
This gives states the power
to say how a candidate

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:24.220
gets put on the state ballot.

00:10:24.220 --> 00:10:26.480
The courts have confirmed that
the states have a compelling

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:27.870
interest in finalizing the ballot

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:29.250
and establishing deadlines.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:31.960
So far courts have been reluctant
to find any problem with

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:34.060
how the states finalize their ballots.

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:36.650
The states have considerations
such as timely handling

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:39.730
of absentee ballot, military
ballots, vote tabulation,

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.070
and they're allowed to
administratively run elections

00:10:42.070 --> 00:10:43.710
without federal interference.

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:45.970
This is why states have
deadlines for when the parties

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:48.150
must certify their
candidates for the ballot.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:50.600
And here, this is a huge
issue because most ballot

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.260
access deadlines have already passed,

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.160
because they were in August and September.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:56.370
Only a few states have
deadlines in October.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.100
California, New Hampshire,
Rhode Island and Wisconsin.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.400
And a real problem arises
when the candidate dies

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:04.060
in October or November
which is too late to get

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:06.110
on the ballot, but right
before the election.

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:08.870
What happens to the vote
for the deceased candidate?

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.320
Well, we know the answer
when it comes to electing

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:14.650
a member of Congress and
it's potentially instructive

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.470
for a presidential candidate.

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.150
There are two main ways of handling this.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.480
There's the American Rule
and the British Rule.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.987
The constitution says, "The
House and the Senate each

00:11:22.987 --> 00:11:24.867
"shall be the judge of the elections,

00:11:24.867 --> 00:11:27.740
"returns and qualifications
of its own members."

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:29.760
So Congress gets to decide what happens

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.340
to a dead Congress person's vote.

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:34.210
Congress has always
followed the American Rule,

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.417
which says, "That the votes
given to the dead candidate

00:11:36.417 --> 00:11:37.400
"are not void."

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:39.820
Those votes are considered
valid and if the dead person

00:11:39.820 --> 00:11:42.080
receives the most votes,
this creates a vacancy

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:43.280
in the office.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.780
That vacancy will be filled
according to the constitution.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.270
And vacancies in the
Senate are filled in accord

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:50.180
with the 17th Amendment,
which permits governors

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.380
to temporarily appoint
someone to the Senate seat.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.610
And here's an example how
that works in the real world,

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:57.300
Missouri governor, Mel Carnahan
won election to the Senate

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:00.450
in Missouri in 2000, 38
days after he and his son

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:01.800
died in a plane crash.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:03.090
Under Missouri Election law,

00:12:03.090 --> 00:12:05.550
the names of deceased
candidates remain on the ballot

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.070
if they die so close to the
election of the filing deadline

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:10.360
or the time for political
parties to substitute

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.820
a candidate has passed.

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:14.390
And Missouri law expressly
follows the American Rule,

00:12:14.390 --> 00:12:16.950
which means that Carnahan's
death and the subsequent

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:18.890
victory created a vacancy.

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:20.560
Carnahan's death created two vacancies.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.150
One in the Senate and also
one as Missouri Governor.

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.480
Roger Wilson, ascended
to the acting governor

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:28.750
upon Carnahan's death
and he announced that

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:31.130
he would appoint Jean Carnahan, Mel's wife

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:32.430
to the Senate seat.

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:34.850
House vacancies are also
handled according to State law,

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:37.130
but governors must provide
for a special election

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:39.620
rather than appointing
someone on an interim basis.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.810
And this is what happened
when two Democratic members

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:43.740
of the House, Hale Boggs of Louisiana

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:48.340
and Nick Begich of Alaska were
killed on October 16th, 1972.

00:12:48.340 --> 00:12:50.840
Under Louisiana and Alaska
law, it was too late

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:52.260
to replace them on the ballot.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.670
Both deceased candidates won
their races and their seats

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:57.400
were eventually filled
by a special election.

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.480
But not everyone likes the
rule that dead candidates

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:00.980
can win elections.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:03.120
The Minority Rule known
as the British Rule

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:06.260
would give the election to
the next highest vote getter.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:07.970
This means that the
person who finished second

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:10.340
would be seated in the House or Senate.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:12.847
This rule has not been followed
by either House of Congress.

00:13:12.847 --> 00:13:15.900
And some argue that the
election of a dead person

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.840
should be considered a
nullity and we should just

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:21.440
have a new election to
fill the seat regardless.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.470
The argument for the British
Rule is that the Senate

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.650
and House have a right to
judge the qualifications

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:27.580
of its members, and a person who is dead

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:29.150
can't be properly qualified.

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:31.230
Therefore the election
should at least be a nullity.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.640
Congress has been reluctant
to adopt this British Rule

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:35.250
because it invalidates
the will of the people

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:36.320
in a popular election.

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:38.530
And it would arguably conflict
with a lot of State laws

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:41.030
which follow the American
Rule with their own precedent

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:42.430
for handling vacancies.

00:13:42.430 --> 00:13:44.540
But this still doesn't tell
us precisely what would happen

00:13:44.540 --> 00:13:47.960
if a presidential candidate
died in a Federal Presidential

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:49.720
election, when it was too late for them

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.760
to be replaced on the
ballots for all 50 States.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:54.650
Moreover, the death of
a presidential candidate

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:55.890
does not create a vacancy.

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.910
So we need to look at how
presidents are elected.

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:01.420
So the question is what happens
if a presidential candidate

00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:03.110
dies right before the election?

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:05.410
When voters cast their
votes on November 3rd,

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:07.730
they're not actually casting
votes for a specific person.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:09.870
Instead they're casting
votes for a slate of electors

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:11.920
who will be sent to the Electoral College.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:13.660
That makes the presidential
election different

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:15.720
from a Senate or House election.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:18.000
And we can argue until the cows come home

00:14:18.000 --> 00:14:20.160
about whether the Electoral
College is a good thing

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:21.110
or a bad thing?

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.200
But it's the system that we have.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.260
So now let's assume
that the president dies

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.150
two days before the election.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.720
There's not a time for the courts

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.280
to resolve the ballot access problem.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:32.590
The election is happening
with his name on the ballot

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:36.120
in some states and Mike Pence's
name on the ballot in states

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:38.180
that allow him for a substitution.

00:14:38.180 --> 00:14:40.340
And the first thing to know is
that if the president passes

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:43.100
away under the 25th Amendment,
then the vice president

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:45.090
becomes president no matter
what, because the rules

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:47.480
of succession established
by the constitution.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.060
And if Biden won the
election, then there's

00:14:49.060 --> 00:14:49.893
not really a problem.

00:14:49.893 --> 00:14:52.290
Biden would be sworn in, in 2021.

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:54.450
And Pence would be president until then.

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:58.250
But what if Joe Biden dies
and then subsequently wins?

00:14:58.250 --> 00:14:59.970
Does Kamala Harris become president?

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:02.610
And what if the combined
Trump's Pence ticket

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:05.110
receive 270 electoral votes, meaning that

00:15:05.110 --> 00:15:06.210
the ticket won the election?

00:15:06.210 --> 00:15:08.910
Does Trump get to be
sworn in for a new term?

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:11.750
Well, the answer lies with
the Electoral College.

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:13.910
So each state's electoral votes usually go

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:15.810
to the winner of the state's popular vote.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:18.200
Some states allow electors to
vote for anyone they choose.

00:15:18.200 --> 00:15:20.600
About 30 States require
the electorates to be bound

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:22.090
by the state's popular vote.

00:15:22.090 --> 00:15:24.330
14 of the 30 States have laws mandating

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.090
that the electoral vote be canceled

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:27.450
if the person goes rogue.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:29.760
This is called being,
"A faithless elector."

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.930
However, some States
simply penalize the elector

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:33.890
with a fine or short jail term.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:36.110
But their vote can still
be cast for someone

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:37.940
other than the popular vote winner.

00:15:37.940 --> 00:15:40.690
Few states actually have laws
specifying what electors do

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:43.570
if a candidate dies before
the Electoral College meets.

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:46.027
Indiana law says that, "Electors
should switch their votes

00:15:46.027 --> 00:15:48.320
"to the political parties
replacement candidate."

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:51.750
So if a deceased candidate
Trump wins under Indiana law,

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:53.480
the electoral votes would go to whomever

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.190
the Republican party replaces him with

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:57.890
which may be Mike Pence, but
it could be somebody else.

00:15:57.890 --> 00:15:59.740
In contrast, Michigan
law requires electors

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:02.870
to vote for the winning candidates
who appear on the ballot.

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:04.780
Presumably this means that they must vote

00:16:04.780 --> 00:16:07.270
for the vice presidential
candidate on the ballot.

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:09.490
The electors may simply cast
their votes for the person

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:11.030
the winning political party has put up

00:16:11.030 --> 00:16:12.440
as the replacement candidate.

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:14.730
Or an elector could believe
that they should cast

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:16.840
their vote for the dead
candidate because the state law

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.130
commands it or for any other reason.

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:21.830
An elector could do that even
though there is precedent

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:24.460
for Congress disallowing the
vote for a deceased candidate.

00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:26.500
But what the state parties
and electors have to do

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:29.060
in this event is largely
governed by State law.

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:31.570
And as usual LawFare
has done the hard work

00:16:31.570 --> 00:16:34.060
and taken on the herculean
effort of compiling

00:16:34.060 --> 00:16:35.920
all of the law of the
states that are likely

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:36.920
to be in play here.

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:39.480
So if you're curious about
what the various State laws

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:42.290
mandate during a vacancy,
definitely check out LawFare.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:45.690
In the 1872 election,
Congress decided not to count

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:48.070
three electoral votes for
presidential candidate

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:51.250
Horace Greeley, who had died
after the November election

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:53.700
but before the meeting
of the Electoral College.

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.820
Would Congress follow that precedent?

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:57.480
It's impossible to say.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:16:59.810
If one party controlled
both Houses of Congress,

00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:01.960
then it'd be easier for them to invalidate

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:04.450
the electoral votes of
the deceased candidate.

00:17:04.450 --> 00:17:07.690
But the Electoral College does
not vote until December 14th

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:10.360
of this year, over a
month after the election.

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:12.240
If a candidate dies between the election

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:13.770
and the Electoral College voting,

00:17:13.770 --> 00:17:16.170
the process is the same
that I talked about before.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.400
The party seeks a replacement candidate

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:20.740
and the party would probably
expect their electors

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:23.250
to still vote for their new
candidate, but they're not

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:24.780
necessarily required to do so.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:26.680
It depends on the State
law and they could vote

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:29.400
for another member of their
party or even a third party

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:30.760
candidate if they wanted to.

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:32.730
And then of course it
gets even more complicated

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:34.980
if no candidate can be certified to have

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:38.600
270 Electoral College votes
under the 12th Amendment.

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:41.050
Which we'll talk about in just a second.

00:17:41.050 --> 00:17:42.933
So then there's the question
of what if a president-elect

00:17:42.933 --> 00:17:44.740
dies after the Electoral College meets,

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:46.660
but before being inaugurated?

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:48.490
Congress will count the
Electoral college votes

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:50.650
on January 6, 2021.

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:52.660
They must then certify that vote.

00:17:52.660 --> 00:17:54.570
And if a winning candidate
were to die in between

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:57.130
December 15th, after
the electors have voted

00:17:57.130 --> 00:18:00.680
but before January 6th, things
start to get pretty messy.

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:02.240
There isn't a Federal
law that says whether

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:04.250
the electoral votes of
the deceased candidate

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.160
also count for the vice president.

00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:08.717
The constitution's 20th Amendment
says, "The vice president

00:18:08.717 --> 00:18:10.937
"elect becomes president
if the president elect

00:18:10.937 --> 00:18:12.920
"dies before inauguration day."

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.537
But it also provides
that, "If the president

00:18:14.537 --> 00:18:17.047
"has not qualified by January 20th,

00:18:17.047 --> 00:18:19.377
"then the vice president elect
serves as acting president

00:18:19.377 --> 00:18:21.290
"until a president qualifies."

00:18:21.290 --> 00:18:23.940
The vice president elect who
is subsequently inaugurated

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:26.280
as president would then
nominate a vice president

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:29.030
under provisions in Section
2 of the 25th Amendment.

00:18:29.030 --> 00:18:31.870
But that might not actually
resolve the problem here.

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:34.510
Does a candidate formally
become the president elect

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:36.360
after winning the Electoral College vote?

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:39.080
Or only after Congress certifies the vote?

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:41.480
This is a question that
has remained unanswered.

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:44.200
However, in the 1932 House
Committee Report accompanying

00:18:44.200 --> 00:18:46.550
the 20th Amendment, it actually
explains that the authors

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:48.700
of the Amendment felt that
the counting of the votes

00:18:48.700 --> 00:18:51.060
does not matter when
determining if a person

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:53.157
is president elect.

00:18:53.157 --> 00:18:55.127
"It will be noted that the
committee uses the term

00:18:55.127 --> 00:18:57.327
"President elect" in its
generally accepted sense

00:18:57.327 --> 00:18:59.617
"as meaning the person who
has received the majority

00:18:59.617 --> 00:19:02.247
"of electoral votes, or the
person who has been chosen

00:19:02.247 --> 00:19:04.077
"by the House of Representatives
in the event that

00:19:04.077 --> 00:19:06.387
"the election is thrown into the House.

00:19:06.387 --> 00:19:08.507
"It is immaterial whether or not the votes

00:19:08.507 --> 00:19:10.837
"have been counted, for the person becomes

00:19:10.837 --> 00:19:14.110
"the President elect as
soon as the votes are cast."

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:16.250
This still isn't the clean
resolution that we were

00:19:16.250 --> 00:19:19.400
probably hoping for in a
crazy hypothetical like this.

00:19:19.400 --> 00:19:21.290
If Congress says it
cannot certify the count

00:19:21.290 --> 00:19:23.440
for a dead candidate, for whatever reason

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:25.050
the Congress chooses.

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:27.160
Then the House of Representatives
picks the president

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:28.870
according to the 12th Amendment.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:31.160
Under the 12th Amendment, if
no can candidate for president

00:19:31.160 --> 00:19:34.080
receives a majority of the
Electoral College votes

00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:36.140
then the House gets to decide.

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:38.970
And the House must choose from
the top three vote getters.

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:40.780
But this means that in a two horse race,

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:43.810
such as what we have this
year, then effectively

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:46.120
the living candidate would
then be become president.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:49.160
But as you can imagine this
whole process would be insanely

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.410
political and basically chaos.

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:54.190
And it's not entirely clear,
but if the president elect

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:56.240
were to die after Congress has certified

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:58.700
the Electoral College vote on January 6th,

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:00.550
then the most likely scenario is that

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:02.370
the rules of succession would apply.

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:04.070
Meaning the vice president elect

00:20:04.070 --> 00:20:06.060
would then become president.

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:08.040
But this whole situation
sounds like a recipe

00:20:08.040 --> 00:20:09.350
for absolute chaos.

00:20:09.350 --> 00:20:11.657
And I think I speak for most
Americans when I say that,

00:20:11.657 --> 00:20:15.327
"Hopefully this is a normal
election between two healthy

00:20:15.327 --> 00:20:18.980
"individuals and the winner
wins fair and square."

00:20:18.980 --> 00:20:21.610
But at the end of the day,
if things do go pear shaped,

00:20:21.610 --> 00:20:23.780
it's basically a matter of State law.

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:26.410
But those laws only come into
play if the election is close.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.100
So let's make this election a blow out

00:20:28.100 --> 00:20:30.260
and avoid another Bush versus Gore.

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.590
So call your friends and
family and tell them to vote

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:34.690
absentee or in person if they can.

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:37.410
And the best way to make that
